    "knowledge_point_id": "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/8b237f9e857b1ed87de447aa79c14d359429e71d8afe7ab8eab201eba7ffb6af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B+2D?$",
    "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Therefore, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+2D=2+2\\cdot (1)=2+2=4.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)+1.\n$$",
